Exercise 1.23 assumes a countable neighbourhood system \(D = \{X_0, X_1, \ldots\}\) whose finite consistency test is decidable, defines \(Y_0 = X_0\) and \(Y_{n+1} = X_{n+1}\) when that set is consistent with \(Y_0, \ldots, Y_n\) and \(Y_{n+1} = Y_n\) otherwise, and asks to show that \(\{Y_n \mid n \in \mathbb{N}\}\) is a total element and that every filter is determined by a sequence of neighbourhoods, after first checking that every finite prefix is consistent. The gloss proves each of those claims: `Y_mem` says every term is a neighbourhood, by the enumeration at zero and, at a successor, either the next enumerated neighbourhood or the previous term; `Y_prefix_consistent` says every finite prefix is consistent because its finite intersection is a neighbourhood contained in itself; membership of each term and of each running intersection in the greedy element is the containment of that intersection in the term; `greedyElement_isTotal` says a filter extending the greedy element cannot contain an enumerated neighbourhood that was rejected, because the previous running intersection and that neighbourhood would then have a neighbourhood as their intersection; `filters_sequence_determined` says every filter is the limit family of the sequence that selects the enumerated neighbourhood when the filter contains it and the master otherwise. Decidable membership of an arbitrary set decides the consistency test, because a finite sequence is consistent exactly when its intersection is a neighbourhood. The closing sentence of the gloss nevertheless says that the numbered name `example_123` is the statement that each term is a neighbourhood. The exported theorem is `Y_prefix_consistent`. **Theorem:** partial — totality of the greedy element and sequential determination of filters are argued in the gloss, but `example_123` is prefix consistency, the hint, and the gloss names the wrong statement. **Proof:** partial — the prefix, rejection, and limit-family arguments match the exercise; they are not the numbered theorem.
